  "slug": "framework-package-2b71",
  "summary": "Target-free allele reconstruction from nanopore amplicons and tree-based haplotype modeling are now being validated on real and simulated genomic datasets.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational inference of allelic and haplotype structure from sequence data, with the two preprints addressing different levels of resolution. A de novo, sequence-first algorithm reconstructs alleles from Oxford Nanopore amplicon reads without requiring target input, and is validated on 20 Coriell CYP2D6 samples. TBLUP, a local DNA tree-based method, estimates haplotype and mutation effects and is shown on simulated and cattle mtDNA data. The new elements are target-free allele reconstruction from long reads and tree-based local modeling of haplotype effects. The UK Biobank whole-genome study of 347,630 individuals is a population-scale heritability analysis and is included here as a separate published reference point rather than a method of the same class. The items do not establish a shared benchmark or ground-truth framework across methods.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-11-13"
}
